WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.847
Empty sets create an important edge case.

00:00:02.847 --> 00:00:06.047
ANY needs at least one successful comparison.

00:00:06.047 --> 00:00:11.752
If the set is empty, no comparison can succeed, so ANY is FALSE.

00:00:11.752 --> 00:00:15.277
ALL asks whether every comparison succeeds.

00:00:15.277 --> 00:00:18.747
With no values, there is no failing comparison, so ALL is TRUE.

00:00:18.747 --> 00:00:26.647
This can feel surprising, but it follows the logic of existential and universal conditions.

00:00:26.647 --> 00:00:32.337
Connect this idea to a compliance workflow checking whether related evidence exists.

00:00:32.337 --> 00:00:37.782
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:37.782 --> 00:00:45.132
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:45.132 --> 00:00:50.767
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:56.548
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
